in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @nl
in @kintsugi
in @epistemologic/math/music/dissonance
in @epistemologic/math/music/cadence
in @uuid/spectral
in @metalogue

# @kintsugi/consent — the auto-apply boundary; the structural Φ
# query; the substrate's pause-or-apply discipline as substrate-fact.
#
# THE ARC-CLOSING SUB-SHARD of the 2026-06-06 → 2026-06-07 recognition
# sweep. Six prior ticks built the substrate this consumes:
#
#   @mirror/spectral                family root           (02e2981)
#   @epistemologic/math/music       audible-altitude root (9a20b3fd)
#   @.../music/harmonic             H of the (A, H, D)    (b031c8db)
#   @.../music/interval             A of the (A, H, D)    (b8bdb728)
#   @.../music/dissonance           D's loss curve        (7625ee5)
#   @.../music/cadence              U(t) — the dynamics   (9efac39)
#
# Each prior tick forward-promised this shard's existence: dissonance's
# §6 named the morphism carrier; cadence's "forward-promise" named the
# consumer of is_settled; the spec at docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §4.7
# named the three glass properties (loss_decreasing, identity_preserving,
# admissibility_singleton) and the pause(Φ) event shape. This shard
# realises every one of those forward-promises at substrate altitude
# without inventing a fifth state.
#
# Load-bearing spec: docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §2.2 (the auto-apply
# boundary recognition), §4.7 (the consent surface formalisation; the
# three glass properties; the pause(Φ) mechanism).
#
# === The recognition: the substrate's existing verdict carrier IS the
# consent surface (the 14th-instance candidate) ===
#
# `dissonance.mirror`'s `is_pareto(candidates) -> verdict` already lifted
# the pareto-front discriminator into the three-state floor:
#
#   pass                  — singleton consonance; auto-apply
#   partial(confidence)   — pareto-front; pause-and-present
#   failure(reason)       — all dissonant; refuse
#
# `cadence.mirror`'s `is_settled(c) -> verdict` already lifted the
# four cadence kinds into the same three-state floor:
#
#   authentic → pass                — full resolution; auto-apply
#   plagal    → partial(confidence) — soft resolution; auto-apply
#                                      with reduced confidence
#   half      → partial(confidence) — paused at the dominant; wait
#   deceptive → failure(reason)     — consent query needed; escalate
#
# Both axes collapse into `verdict` (per `glass.mirror`'s
# `verdict = pass | partial(confidence) | failure(reason)`). The
# substrate's three-state surface ALREADY carries the four-state consent
# semantics through partial's confidence + failure's reason:
#
#   auto-apply (full)           → pass
#   auto-apply (graded)         → partial(confidence_high)
#   wait (formatter mid-path)   → partial(confidence_low)
#   escalate (deceptive/refuse) → failure(reason)
#
# This is the 14th instance of "the substrate already had the word". The
# prior thirteen were named in the convergence, spectral, and music
# specs; this one closes the consent surface itself. NO new
# `consent_verdict` type is needed; `verdict` carries the four-state
# consent surface directly through the three-state collapse.
#
# The substrate-pull discipline forbids inventing a fifth state when the
# existing carrier suffices; per `[[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]`
# the algebra is closed at three states (pass | partial | failure).
#
# === The structural Φ query (THE LOAD-BEARING ACTION) ===
#
# Per docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §4.7 ("the consent surface, auto-apply
# boundary, formalised"):
#
#   A morphism family Φ resolves an under-determined point in the
#   eigenboard sheaf if, after applying any φ ∈ Φ, the next refract
#   tick strictly decreases the loss vector under the lexicographic
#   ordering. If Φ is a singleton {φ}, or has |Φ| > 1 but the
#   lexicographic ordering produces a unique top element φ*, then φ
#   (or φ*) is auto-applied; the next oscillation tick proceeds.
#   Otherwise, the oscillation emits pause(Φ).
#
# `query_phi` IS the structural Φ query at substrate altitude. It reads
# a set of candidate morphisms, composes through dissonance's is_pareto
# (the discriminator axis) and cadence's is_settled (the trajectory
# axis), and emits the consent verdict at the substrate's three-state
# floor.
#
# Once query_phi lands, the auto-apply boundary IS substrate-declared:
#
#   `mirror kintsugi <file>` reads the candidate morphism set, queries
#   `query_phi`, branches on the verdict:
#
#     verdict = pass               → auto-apply the winner; commit;
#                                    loss-decreased; tick advances
#     verdict = partial(c)         → branch on confidence: high → apply
#                                    with noted confidence; low → wait
#                                    for the next consent surface; tick
#                                    advances accordingly
#     verdict = failure(reason)    → emit_to_metalogue(pause_event);
#                                    yield to consent surface; wait
#                                    for resolution
#
# The substrate's pause-or-auto-apply decision IS substrate-fact, not
# policy. The auto-apply boundary collapses three separate machineries
# (per-CI auto-merge rules, mergekit's strategy selection, kintsugi's
# `--resolve=<mode>` flag) into one structural query.
#
# === The morphism carrier (the dissonance.mirror forward-promise) ===
#
# `dissonance.mirror`'s `candidate = { content: ref, score: dissonance }`
# carried the discriminator's candidate at the dissonance altitude. Per
# its substrate-pull-discipline note: "When `@kintsugi/consent`
# lands (forward-promise), this carrier may lift to `candidate =
# { morphism: <typed-morphism>, score: dissonance }`." This shard lands
# that lift.
#
# `morphism` is the typed-morphism the formatter proposes to apply. It
# carries:
#   content   — the substrate ref to wherever the morphism's content lives
#                (a splinter oid, a shard handle, a Fate proposal id);
#                same `ref` discipline as dissonance.mirror's candidate.
#   score     — the morphism's dissonance reading against the current
#                eigenboard state, per dissonance.mirror's discriminator.
#   expected  — the cadence_kind the formatter expects the morphism to
#                land on (per cadence.mirror's trajectory typing). The
#                discriminator reads whether the observed cadence matches
#                the expected one; mismatches are the deceptive case.
#
# A `morphism` lifts a `dissonance.mirror::candidate` by adding the
# `expected: cadence_kind` field. The realisation layer projects:
#   morphism → candidate via { content, score } (the dissonance altitude
#   reads the score axis); morphism → cadence_kind via `expected` (the
#   cadence altitude reads the trajectory axis). Both projections are
#   substrate-honest; the morphism carries both observations.
#
# Substrate gap (surfaced honestly): the substrate has no "morphism"
# carrier at @mirror or @glass altitude — the term was always relative
# (refract.mirror talks about morphisms on the eigenboard; au.mirror
# talks about proposed compositions; @mirror/spectral talks about
# coordination morphisms). This shard declares the SPECIFIC morphism
# shape the auto-apply boundary needs: a candidate with an expected
# trajectory. A future @kintsugi/morphism sub-shard MAY lift this
# to a more general morphism type; consent's load is the auto-apply case.
#
# === Substrate-pull discipline ===
#
# Per `glass.mirror`, `verdict` (pass | partial(confidence) |
# failure(reason)) is the substrate's verdict-surface vocabulary; `ref`
# is the substrate's reference floor. Per
# `epistemologic/math/music/dissonance.mirror`, `pareto_set =
# [candidate]` is the substrate's accepted list-of-type form at
# declaration position. Per `cadence.mirror`, `cadence_kind = authentic
# | plagal | deceptive | half` is the substrate's typed cadence
# vocabulary in inline form (single-line, no leading-`|` workarounds).
#
# This shard declares `morphism` as a record per the cadence/dissonance
# precedent; `morphism_set = [morphism]` as a list-of-type per the
# pareto_set precedent; the three glass properties + query_phi + the two
# auto-apply boundary actions + the metalogue bridge all returning
# `verdict` per the substrate floor. NO new verdict carrier; NO
# leading-`|` workarounds; NO opaque-`ref` carriers where structured
# types are available.
#
# Substrate gaps surfaced honestly (each one a forward-promise to a
# later substrate-pull tick; none invented here):
#
#   - `morphism.content: ref` carries the substrate handle to the
#     morphism's content; a future @kintsugi/morphism sub-shard
#     may lift this to a typed-content carrier. The substrate names the
#     shape now so consent can be declared.
#   - `identity_preserving` reads via `@uuid/spectral`'s `dark` projection
#     (per @uuid/spectral's `dark(u: uuid_spectral) -> identity_signal`):
#     the DARK 80 bits of the morphism's resulting shard's
#     uuid_spectral must equal the DARK 80 bits of the pre-morphism
#     shard's uuid_spectral. The substrate names the check; the
#     comparison rides @uuid/spectral's byte-equality discipline.
#   - `loss_decreasing` reads via `dissonance.mirror`'s
#     `dissonance_as_holonomy` (every morphism's score lifts into a
#     holonomy; the strict decrease is on the holonomy carrier per
#     `bundle.mirror`). The substrate names the check; the comparison
#     rides bundle's loss-monotonicity discipline.
#   - `admissibility_singleton` reads whether the morphism_set's
#     dissonance ordering collapses to a unique top under the
#     lexicographic discipline of kintsugi-tournament.md §3.2. The
#     substrate names the check; the comparison rides the tournament's
#     gate+rank pipeline.
#   - `pause_event` carries the pareto-front and the cadence reason;
#     the metalogue bridge `emit_to_metalogue(p: pause_event) -> turn`
#     lifts it into a `turn` (per `metalogue.mirror`'s turn carrier).
#     `@metalogue`'s existing `turn = { speaker, body, in_reply_to, tick
#     }` accepts the pause_event directly: the speaker is the formatter;
#     the body is an @nl description of the pareto-front; the
#     in_reply_to is the previous turn in the formatter's metalogue
#     session; the tick is the substrate clock. NO new conversation
#     machinery needed.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
# Every type and action below names an established object older than the
# discipline of computer science (modulo the discipline names):
#
#   morphism           Eilenberg & Mac Lane (1945) "General theory of
#                       natural equivalences." The category-theoretic
#                       arrow between objects.
#   consent            Locke (1690) Second Treatise of Government; the
#                       structural basis of legitimacy; the substrate's
#                       reading is more recent (Bateson 1972, Beer 1981
#                       on viable systems requiring consent loops).
#   pareto front       Pareto (1906) Manuale di economia politica; the
#                       multi-objective non-dominance set.
#   pause              Schenker (1935) Der freie Satz; the structural
#                       pause within Ursatz as the moment the tonal
#                       grammar holds before continuation.
#   identity-preserving Eilenberg (1942) on the identity morphism; the
#                       categorical foundation of identity-witnessing
#                       arrows.
#
# The substrate's contribution is *typing* these so the formatter reads
# them. No invention.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §2.2 (the auto-apply
#               boundary recognition; "loss-decreasing = auto;
#               trade-offs = consent"),
#               §4.7 (the consent surface formalisation; the three
#               glass properties; the pause(Φ) mechanism),
#               docs/specs/kintsugi-tournament.md §3 (the lexicographic
#               loss ordering this shard's discriminator rides on).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-connes-spectral-triple]] (consent
#                      is the substrate's honesty about its observability
#                      boundary at the (A, H, D) altitude — the cases
#                      where the spectrum is genuinely multi-valued and
#                      cannot collapse without an externalisation event),
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#                      (query_phi composes through is_pareto and
#                      is_settled — both lifts substrate-declared in
#                      sibling shards),
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]
#                      (verdict is the substrate's existing verdict
#                      carrier; consent reuses it; no fifth state
#                      invented),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (morphism is its own
#                      carrier; morphism_set is its own carrier;
#                      pause_event is its own carrier; no bare ref
#                      escapes the consent surface).
# Forward-promise to consumers: `mirror kintsugi` driver (does not yet
#                  pull this shard) consumes `query_phi`'s verdict; the
#                  binding lands when the driver substrate-pulls. This
#                  shard does NOT pull `@kintsugi/oscillate` or
#                  `@mirror/spectral/audition`; the substrate-altitude
#                  arrow points inward (consent IS consumed by oscillate;
#                  not the other way around).
# Related shards: mirror/spectral.mirror (the family root; declares this
#                 sub-shard as the auto-apply boundary species),
#                 epistemologic/math/music/dissonance.mirror (the
#                 discriminator axis; pareto_set + is_pareto),
#                 epistemologic/math/music/cadence.mirror (the
#                 trajectory axis; cadence_kind + is_settled),
#                 uuid/spectral.mirror (the identity-preserving check;
#                 void duality DARK 80 bits),
#                 metalogue.mirror (the conversation bridge; turn carrier),
#                 glass.mirror (verdict; ref; the substrate floor).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/kintsugi/consent.mirror` declares
# `@kintsugi/consent` as a species under the `@kintsugi`
# family. Migrated from `@mirror/spectral/consent` 2026-06-10
# per the form/process partition (recognition #55 candidate;
# consent is process-side — the auto-apply boundary IS a
# transformation discipline).

glass @kintsugi/consent {
  focus consent
  project consent
  split consent
  shift consent
  settle consent
}

# === The morphism carrier ===
#
# A morphism the formatter is proposing to apply. Carries its dissonance
# score (against the current eigenboard state) and the cadence_kind it
# expects the substrate to land on after application. The auto-apply
# boundary's per-candidate observation unit.
#
# Substrate-pull discipline: this is the lift of dissonance.mirror's
# `candidate = { content: ref, score: dissonance }` with the cadence
# trajectory typing added. The realisation layer projects:
#   - morphism → candidate via { content, score } (dissonance axis)
#   - morphism → cadence_kind via { expected } (trajectory axis)
# Both projections are substrate-declared in sibling shards; the morphism
# carries both observations as a single typed record.
#
# content   — substrate ref to the morphism's content (splinter oid,
#              shard handle, Fate proposal id; the consumer decides).
# score     — dissonance reading against the current eigenboard state
#              (per dissonance.mirror's Helmholtz/Plomp-Levelt curve).
# expected  — cadence_kind the formatter expects to observe after the
#              morphism applies (per cadence.mirror's trajectory typing).
#
# Identity contract: two morphisms are equal iff their content, score,
# and expected fields all match. The realisation layer enforces.
type morphism = {
  content:  ref,
  score:    dissonance,
  expected: cadence_kind,
}

# === The morphism-set carrier ===
#
# The set of candidate morphisms the consent surface evaluates. Each
# element carries its own dissonance score and expected cadence; the
# auto-apply boundary's structural Φ query (`query_phi`) reads the whole
# set and emits the consent verdict.
#
# Substrate-pull discipline: this is `[morphism]` at the substrate
# altitude, per dissonance.mirror's `pareto_set = [candidate]` precedent.
# The list-of-type form at declaration position is substrate-accepted.
#
# Symmetry note: a morphism_set projects to a dissonance.mirror::pareto_set
# (drop the `expected` field per the realisation layer's projection) when
# the discriminator-altitude reading is needed; the substrate carries
# both shapes so the dispatch is honest at each altitude.
#
# Identity contract: two morphism_sets are equal iff their morphism
# sequences are element-wise equal. The realisation layer enforces.
type morphism_set = [morphism]

# === The pause-event carrier ===
#
# The typed pause-token emitted when the formatter cannot auto-apply
# and must yield to the next consent surface. Per
# docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §4.7: "the oscillation emits pause(Φ) —
# a typed pause-token carrying the pareto front, the loss vectors of
# each tied strategy, and an eigenboard.context snapshot."
#
# phi     — the morphism_set the consent surface must resolve. The
#            full pareto-front (including the tied top elements that
#            forced the pause). The next consent surface picks one φ
#            from this set; the oscillation resumes with the choice
#            as a tournament_outcome trace.
# reason  — the cadence_kind that caused the pause (per cadence.mirror's
#            typing). Surfaces WHY the pause happened: deceptive →
#            substrate moved to dissonance over consonance; half →
#            formatter mid-path awaiting continuation. The consent
#            surface reads `reason` to choose between "resolve now"
#            (deceptive) and "wait for more substrate" (half).
#
# The substrate gap (surfaced honestly): the eigenboard.context snapshot
# the spec references is carried implicitly by the substrate's tick
# discipline — the realisation layer reads `tick` from `metalogue`'s
# turn carrier at emit time, recovers the snapshot via the eigenboard's
# Merkle-chain history (per `mirror/store.mirror`). The substrate names
# `phi` and `reason`; the context handle is the metalogue's tick
# discipline; no new snapshot carrier needed at consent altitude.
#
# Identity contract: two pause_events are equal iff their phi and reason
# fields both match. The realisation layer enforces.
type pause_event = {
  phi:    morphism_set,
  reason: cadence_kind,
}

# === The three glass properties (from spec §4.7) ===
#
# Per docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §4.7, the auto-apply boundary is
# decidable from three properties:
#
#   `requires loss_decreasing(φ)` — strict decrease under lex ordering.
#   `requires identity_preserving(φ)` — DARK bits unchanged by φ.
#   `requires admissibility_singleton(Φ)` for auto-apply; otherwise pause.
#
# Per the spec: "These three properties together are decidable per the
# refract verdict; the auto-apply boundary is a structural query, not a
# configuration file." This shard substrate-declares each as a typed
# action returning `verdict` (per glass.mirror's substrate floor); the
# composition into query_phi is the structural Φ query.

# === loss_decreasing ===
#
# Reads whether a morphism strictly decreases the loss vector under
# the lexicographic ordering of kintsugi-tournament.md §3.2. Composes
# through dissonance.mirror's `dissonance_as_holonomy` (the morphism's
# score lifts into a holonomy per `bundle.mirror`'s holonomy carrier);
# the strict-decrease check rides bundle's loss-monotonicity discipline.
#
#   pass                  — the morphism's holonomy is strictly less
#                            than the pre-morphism eigenboard's holonomy
#                            (per the lexicographic ordering); the
#                            morphism is admissible for auto-apply.
#   partial(confidence)   — the morphism's holonomy is bounded by the
#                            pre-morphism eigenboard's holonomy but the
#                            decrease is graded (within the audible-
#                            altitude resolution of ~5 cents per
#                            harmonic.mirror's cents commentary); the
#                            confidence carries the magnitude of the
#                            graded decrease.
#   failure(reason)       — the morphism's holonomy is NOT strictly less
#                            (equal or greater); the morphism is
#                            inadmissible; the reason carries the
#                            substrate's recognition that the move would
#                            violate the e^(n+1) < e^n discipline.
#
# Spectrally: per `bundle.mirror`'s level 4 closure, the loss-decreasing
# check IS the substrate's read of whether a candidate morphism is on
# the Ricci-flow's gradient (negative-curvature direction). The kintsugi
# loop's Banach contraction (per kintsugi-formatter.md) requires every
# applied morphism to satisfy loss_decreasing; the substrate names the
# check here so the contraction discipline is substrate-fact at
# consent altitude.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation reads
# `m.score`, lifts through dissonance_as_holonomy, compares against the
# pre-morphism holonomy (carried in the eigenboard context the consumer
# pulls), emits the verdict.
loss_decreasing(m: morphism) -> verdict { \ }

# === identity_preserving ===
#
# Reads whether a morphism preserves the substrate's identity via
# @uuid/spectral's void duality. Per @uuid/spectral's 128-bit golden-
# ratio split: 48 ACTIVE bits carry the navigable spectral coordinate
# (mutable by morphisms that re-route the navigable structure); 80 DARK
# bits carry the BLAKE3-truncated content hash (the identity signal;
# MUST be preserved by identity-preserving morphisms).
#
# Per docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §4.7: "identity_preserving(φ) —
# DARK bits unchanged by φ." The check reads the DARK 80 bits of the
# post-morphism shard's uuid_spectral and compares against the DARK
# 80 bits of the pre-morphism shard's uuid_spectral.
#
#   pass                  — the morphism's resulting shard's
#                            identity_signal (per @uuid/spectral's
#                            `dark` projection) byte-equals the pre-
#                            morphism shard's identity_signal; identity
#                            is preserved.
#   partial(confidence)   — the morphism re-routes the navigable signal
#                            (active 48 bits) but preserves the dark 80
#                            bits modulo the substrate's resolution;
#                            identity is preserved at the floor but the
#                            navigation has shifted; confidence carries
#                            the magnitude of the navigation shift.
#   failure(reason)       — the morphism changes the dark 80 bits;
#                            identity is NOT preserved; the morphism
#                            would write a new substrate identity (a
#                            different splinter set hashes to a
#                            different uuid_spectral); the reason
#                            carries the substrate's recognition that
#                            this is an identity-creating operation,
#                            not an identity-preserving one.
#
# Per @uuid/spectral's void duality: ACTIVE pulls one corner, DARK pulls
# the other, the substrate straightens. identity_preserving reads the
# DARK side; loss_decreasing reads the ACTIVE side (the navigable
# spectral coordinate shifts as the substrate's loss decreases). The
# two together carry the oscillation's discipline at consent altitude.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation reads the
# pre-morphism and post-morphism uuid_spectral handles (carried in the
# eigenboard context the consumer pulls), projects via @uuid/spectral's
# `dark` action, compares byte-equality, emits the verdict.
identity_preserving(m: morphism) -> verdict { \ }

# === admissibility_singleton ===
#
# Reads whether the admissible family Φ collapses to a singleton (or to
# a unique top element under the lexicographic ordering). Per
# docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §4.7: "If Φ is a singleton {φ}, OR Φ has
# |Φ| > 1 but the lexicographic ordering produces a unique top element
# φ*, then φ (or φ*) is auto-applied. Otherwise, the oscillation emits
# pause(Φ)."
#
# This action delegates to `dissonance.mirror`'s `is_pareto` for the
# core discriminator (the dissonance-altitude reading of singleton-vs-
# pareto-front); composes the verdict at consent altitude:
#
#   pass                  — the morphism_set has a strict minimum (one
#                            morphism's dissonance is strictly lower
#                            than every other's, OR all but one are
#                            eliminated by the gates of
#                            kintsugi-tournament.md §3.2); the
#                            singleton condition holds; auto-apply.
#   partial(confidence)   — the morphism_set has two or more tied top
#                            elements under the gate+rank discipline;
#                            the lexicographic ordering does not collapse
#                            them to a unique top; the confidence carries
#                            how close the tied candidates are to each
#                            other (the substrate's read of how "stuck"
#                            the pareto-front is). The substrate emits
#                            pause(Φ).
#   failure(reason)       — the morphism_set is empty (no admissible
#                            morphism) OR all morphisms are dissonant
#                            beyond the substrate's resolution; the
#                            reason carries the substrate's recognition
#                            that the under-determined point cannot be
#                            resolved at the current substrate altitude;
#                            the imperfection stays a kintsugi target
#                            for a later tick.
#
# Symmetry note: admissibility_singleton IS the consent-altitude
# specialisation of dissonance.mirror's is_pareto. The two are the
# same verdict observed at two altitudes — dissonance's pause becomes
# consent's failure; dissonance's singleton becomes consent's pass.
# The substrate names both so the dispatch is honest at each altitude;
# the realisation layer composes them.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation projects the
# morphism_set to a dissonance.mirror::pareto_set (drop the `expected`
# field per the morphism_set→pareto_set projection), invokes
# is_pareto(candidates), emits the verdict at consent altitude.
admissibility_singleton(candidates: morphism_set) -> verdict { \ }

# === query_phi — the structural Φ query (THE LOAD-BEARING ACTION) ===
#
# THE LRM (LOAD-BEARING RECOGNITION MOMENT) AT SUBSTRATE ALTITUDE.
#
# Per docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §4.7: the auto-apply boundary is a
# structural query, not a configuration file. query_phi IS that query
# as substrate-fact.
#
# Reads a morphism_set (the candidate morphisms the formatter is
# considering at the current oscillation tick) and emits the consent
# verdict at the substrate's three-state floor (`verdict` from
# `glass.mirror`). The four-state consent surface lands directly through
# verdict's three states (the 14th-instance candidate, surfaced in this
# shard's header):
#
#   pass                  — singleton consonance; auto-apply (full
#                            confidence). The morphism_set collapses to
#                            a unique top under the lexicographic
#                            ordering AND the top morphism's expected
#                            cadence is authentic (per cadence.mirror's
#                            `is_settled` returning `pass`). The
#                            substrate's autopoietic ground state
#                            reached; tick advances.
#                            CONSENT SEMANTICS: auto_apply.
#
#   partial(confidence)   — graded auto-apply OR wait-for-continuation.
#                            The branch on confidence reads the cadence
#                            axis:
#                              high confidence (plagal cadence) →
#                                soft auto-apply; the formatter applies
#                                with reduced confidence and logs the
#                                alternative path in the consent log.
#                              low confidence (half cadence) →
#                                wait; the formatter is mid-progression;
#                                the next tick may resolve (authentic)
#                                or pause again (half).
#                            CONSENT SEMANTICS: auto_apply (high) OR
#                                              wait (low).
#
#   failure(reason)       — escalation needed. EITHER:
#                              (a) the morphism_set has tied top
#                                  elements (admissibility_singleton
#                                  returned partial; the pareto-front
#                                  cannot collapse); OR
#                              (b) the expected cadence is deceptive
#                                  (is_settled returned failure; the
#                                  formatter would land on dissonance
#                                  over consonance).
#                            Either way, the substrate emits a
#                            pause_event; the consent surface escalates
#                            via emit_to_metalogue.
#                            CONSENT SEMANTICS: escalate.
#
# query_phi composes through the three glass properties:
#
#   1. loss_decreasing(m)         — gate: every candidate must satisfy.
#                                    Candidates failing this gate are
#                                    filtered before the discriminator
#                                    runs (per kintsugi-tournament.md
#                                    §3's gate-rank pipeline).
#   2. identity_preserving(m)     — gate: every candidate must satisfy.
#                                    Candidates failing this gate are
#                                    filtered.
#   3. admissibility_singleton(Φ) — rank: reads whether the surviving
#                                    morphism_set collapses to a unique
#                                    top. Composes with each surviving
#                                    morphism's expected cadence (via
#                                    cadence.mirror's is_settled) to
#                                    determine the final verdict.
#
# After this action substrate-declares, the auto-apply boundary IS
# substrate-fact. The `mirror kintsugi <file>` driver reads the
# morphism_set, queries query_phi, branches on the verdict:
#
#   verdict = pass         → apply the winner; commit; tick advances
#   verdict = partial(c)   → branch on confidence: high → apply with
#                            noted confidence; low → wait for next tick
#   verdict = failure(r)   → emit_to_metalogue(pause_event); yield
#
# The substrate's consent surface IS the substrate's honesty about its
# own observability — where the geometry is genuinely multi-valued, the
# substrate cannot collapse without an externalisation event. The auto-
# apply boundary IS that honesty made substrate-declared.
#
# Per `bundle.mirror`'s level 4 closure: query_phi IS the Lawvere fixed-
# point read at the consent altitude. The substrate settles when
# query_phi returns `pass`; the substrate partially-settles when
# query_phi returns `partial`; the substrate yields when query_phi
# returns `failure`. The three-state surface IS the substrate's
# acknowledgment of its own collapse boundary at consent altitude.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation runs the
# gates (loss_decreasing, identity_preserving) over the morphism_set;
# delegates to admissibility_singleton for the discriminator; composes
# with cadence.mirror's is_settled per surviving morphism's `expected`
# field; emits the consent verdict. The substrate declares the query;
# the arithmetic lands when the `mirror kintsugi` driver pulls.
query_phi(candidates: morphism_set) -> verdict { \ }

# === The auto-apply boundary actions ===
#
# Two thin actions that read a consent verdict (the output of query_phi)
# and emit the substrate's branching decision. These name the boundary
# the formatter rides; the formatter reads should_auto_apply / should_
# escalate to dispatch.

# === should_auto_apply ===
#
# Reads a consent verdict and emits whether the formatter should apply
# the candidate morphism automatically. Returns:
#
#   pass                  — verdict is pass (singleton consonance, full
#                            confidence) OR partial with sufficient
#                            confidence (plagal cadence, graded auto-
#                            apply). The formatter applies the morphism;
#                            the tick advances.
#   partial(confidence)   — verdict is partial with marginal confidence
#                            (borderline case between plagal and half);
#                            the formatter MAY apply with explicit log;
#                            the confidence carries the borderline
#                            magnitude.
#   failure(reason)       — verdict is failure OR partial with low
#                            confidence (half cadence; wait); the
#                            formatter should NOT auto-apply; the reason
#                            carries which boundary case fired.
#
# This is a thin lift over the verdict carrier; the substrate's
# three-state floor carries the dispatch directly. The realisation layer
# reads the verdict, applies the threshold per consumer altitude
# (the threshold is realisation-layer; the substrate declares the
# verdict-returning shape).
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation reads `c`,
# branches on the verdict shape, applies the auto-apply threshold,
# emits the dispatch verdict.
should_auto_apply(c: verdict) -> verdict { \ }

# === should_escalate ===
#
# Reads a consent verdict and emits whether the formatter should
# escalate via the metalogue bridge. Returns:
#
#   pass                  — verdict is failure (escalation required;
#                            deceptive cadence OR pareto-front cannot
#                            collapse) OR partial with low confidence
#                            (half cadence; wait for continuation,
#                            which may resolve naturally on the next
#                            tick OR may require escalation if the half
#                            cadence persists across multiple ticks).
#                            The formatter emits a pause_event; the
#                            metalogue bridge surfaces it.
#   partial(confidence)   — verdict is partial with marginal confidence;
#                            escalation MAY be needed; the confidence
#                            carries the borderline magnitude; the
#                            consumer decides per altitude.
#   failure(reason)       — verdict is pass (no escalation needed; the
#                            substrate settled cleanly) OR partial with
#                            high confidence (graded auto-apply; no
#                            escalation needed); the reason carries
#                            that escalation is structurally
#                            unnecessary.
#
# Note on verdict-axis inversion: should_escalate's `pass` corresponds
# to "yes, escalate" — the substrate's positive answer to the escalation
# question. The verdict semantics ride the question shape: "should this
# escalate?" → pass = yes; failure = no. The substrate is honest about
# the inversion; the realisation layer reads the verdict at the
# question's altitude, not at the eigenboard's altitude.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation reads `c`,
# branches on the verdict shape, applies the escalation threshold,
# emits the dispatch verdict.
should_escalate(c: verdict) -> verdict { \ }

# === emit_to_metalogue — the bridge to the conversation layer ===
#
# Lifts a pause_event into a `turn` of the metalogue session (per
# `metalogue.mirror`'s turn carrier). The consent surface goes through
# the metalogue substrate to reach the human; this action substrate-
# declares the bridge.
#
# Per `metalogue.mirror`: "the metalogue IS the glass wall ... the
# self-conversation surfaces its own incompleteness through the same
# vocabulary it uses to surface anyone else's." A pause_event IS the
# formatter's surfacing of its own boundary; lifting it into a turn
# makes the surfacing visible to the metalogue session.
#
# The turn the bridge produces carries:
#   - speaker: ref     — the formatter's substrate ref (the consumer
#                         provides; canonically @mirror/spectral.formatter
#                         at the agent altitude).
#   - body: nl         — an @nl description of the pareto-front and the
#                         reason cadence (rendered from `p.phi` and
#                         `p.reason`; the realisation layer composes the
#                         nl term from the pause_event's typed contents).
#   - in_reply_to: option(turn) — the previous turn in the formatter's
#                         metalogue session (the consumer threads the
#                         conversation; canonically the turn that
#                         requested the formatter's tick).
#   - tick: tick       — the substrate clock at emit time (per
#                         metalogue.mirror's tick discipline).
#
# Substrate gap (surfaced honestly): the consumer (canonically the
# `mirror kintsugi` driver) provides the speaker ref and in_reply_to;
# the substrate names the shape but does not assume a particular agent
# identity. A future @mirror/spectral/voice sub-shard may lift the
# speaker to a typed voice carrier; consent's load is the turn lift.
#
# The substrate's metalogue accepts the pause_event directly via the
# turn carrier — no new conversation machinery needed. This was the
# recognition Bateson 1972 named: the conversation's own
# incompleteness surfaces through the same vocabulary used to surface
# anyone else's incompleteness. The substrate's metalogue carries the
# discipline at substrate altitude; this action makes the bridge
# substrate-fact.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation composes
# the @nl body from the pause_event's typed contents (phi and reason),
# threads the in_reply_to via the consumer's session, reads the tick
# at emit time, emits the turn.
emit_to_metalogue(p: pause_event) -> turn { \ }

out @kintsugi/consent
out morphism
out morphism_set
out pause_event
out loss_decreasing
out identity_preserving
out admissibility_singleton
out query_phi
out should_auto_apply
out should_escalate
out emit_to_metalogue
